Build Specification — App 24: The Master Workbench (Orchestrator Edition)
Completeness status: COMPLETE. All sections delivered in one pass. No truncation. Formatted for copy-paste. Long lists are broken into short lines.

This is the updated version. It encompasses all 49 apps. It supersedes the original App 24.

1. What It Is
A plugin host, index, and live data orchestrator for all 48 other apps in the suite (Apps 1 through 23, and Apps 25 through 49). It does three things.

One. It hosts all 48 other apps.

Two. It provides a shared parameter registry and a shared data bus, so a value entered in one app automatically flows to every other app that accepts that value.

Three. It runs a dependency graph, so results from one app feed into the next, and the whole suite runs as a chain reaction from a minimum set of user inputs.

It reuses the STAG Schema Library (App 17), the Null-Model Protocol Library (App 18), the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20), the PROV-O Exporter (App 3), the Autofill Library Service (App 49), and every other app in the suite. It is for research groups, labs, consortia, and anyone who uses more than one app.

2. Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Host all 48 other apps as plugins.

Provide a shared parameter registry that defines every parameter used by any app, with its type, its permitted values, its units, and its producing apps and consuming apps.

Provide a shared data bus that carries parameter values between apps.

Provide a dependency graph that defines which app produces which parameter and which app consumes which parameter.

When a user enters a parameter in one app, publish it on the data bus and update every app that consumes it.

When an app produces a result parameter, publish it on the data bus and update every app that consumes it.

Run the chain reaction: when an app receives new input, recompute its output, publish the output, and propagate.

Support manual override: the user can freeze a parameter, so it is not overwritten by the chain reaction.

Support the library (App 23) and the other storage apps as special cases. They store records. They do not participate in the chain reaction as producers or consumers of the statistical parameters, but they can be read from and written to.

Support saving the entire session state: every parameter value, every frozen parameter, every app's state.

Support loading a saved session.

Support exporting the chain of computations: which app produced which parameter, and which app consumed it.

Support a visualisation of the data flow: a graph showing the apps as nodes and the parameter flows as edges.

Support a visualisation of the dependency graph.

Support a "what-if" mode: change one parameter and see the effect propagate through the chain without committing.

Support a "commit" mode: apply the change.

Support a "reset" mode: clear all derived parameters and start from the user inputs.

Support conflict resolution: if two apps produce the same parameter with different values, report the conflict and require the user to choose.

Support the health check for each app.

Support versioning and provenance.

Support a command-line interface and a web interface.

Support the plugin interface so new apps can be added.

Support the shared schema registry.

Support the shared authentication layer.

Support the shared reporting layer.

Support the unified search across all apps.

Support the launcher, the workspace, the navigation, the status view, the configuration view, and the logging view.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic.

Fast: propagate a change through the chain in under three seconds for a typical session.

Scalable: support up to 10^7 records across all apps.

Versioned: the orchestrator records the version of each app and the version of the shared core.

Reproducible.

Portable.

Well-documented.

3. Input Specification
Input 1. Module registry file. Required. Lists the 48 apps, their versions, their dependencies, and their entry points.

Input 2. Parameter registry file. Required. Lists every parameter, its type, its permitted values, its units, its producing apps, and its consuming apps.

Input 3. Dependency graph file. Required. Lists which app produces which parameter and which app consumes which parameter.

Input 4. User-entered parameters. Required for a session. The minimum set of values the user provides.

Input 5. Frozen parameter list. Optional.

Input 6. Session file. Optional.

Input 7. App configuration file. Required for each app.

Input 8. Plugin package. Optional. A new app to add to the registry.

Input 9. Output format. Optional. Values: json, text, html.

4. Output Specification
Output 1. Session state. JSON. Every parameter value, every frozen parameter, every app's state.

Output 2. Parameter registry view. JSON.

Output 3. Data flow graph. JSON or a rendered graph.

Output 4. Dependency graph. JSON or a rendered graph.

Output 5. Chain of computations. JSON.

Output 6. Conflict report. JSON.

Output 7. Audit log. JSON.

Output 8. Reproducibility record. JSON.

Output 9. App registry view. JSON. All 48 apps with their versions.

Output 10. Unified search result. JSON.

Output 11. Backup file. A JSON archive or a database dump.

5. Module-by-Module Specification
Module 1. Module Registry.

Holds the list of registered apps. Each entry has: module_id, module_name, module_version, module_description, module_dependencies, module_entry_point, module_api_endpoint, module_ui_route, module_status, module_configuration_schema, module_health_check.

Module 2. Shared Schema Registry.

Holds the shared schemas. It is the union of the STAG schema (App 17), the Null-Model Protocol schema (App 18), the deposit schema (App 23), and every domain schema registered by the domain apps.

Module 3. Shared Provenance Database.

Holds the provenance records for all apps. It reuses the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20) and extends it with the app identifier.

Module 4. Shared Authentication Layer.

Provides authentication and authorization (role-based access control: reader, depositor, moderator, admin, and app-specific roles).

Module 5. Shared Reporting Layer.

Provides reporting across apps.

Module 6. Unified Search.

Provides unified search across all apps.

Module 7. Parameter Registry.

Holds the parameter registry. Each entry has: parameter_id, parameter_name, parameter_type, parameter_permitted_values, parameter_units, parameter_produced_by, parameter_consumed_by, parameter_default, parameter_description.

Module 8. Data Bus.

Carries parameter values between apps. Each value has: parameter_id, parameter_value, produced_by, produced_at, frozen.

Module 9. Dependency Graph.

Holds the dependency graph. Each node is an app. Each edge is a parameter flow.

Module 10. Orchestrator.

Runs the chain reaction.

Module 11. Conflict Resolver.

Detects and resolves conflicts.

Module 12. Manual Override.

Lets the user freeze parameters.

Module 13. Session Manager.

Saves and loads session state.

Module 14. Chain Reporter.

Produces the chain of computations.

Module 15. Data Flow Visualiser.

Renders the data flow graph.

Module 16. Dependency Graph Visualiser.

Renders the dependency graph.

Module 17. What-If Mode.

Sandbox mode.

Module 18. Commit Mode.

Applies sandbox results.

Module 19. Reset Mode.

Clears derived parameters.

Module 20. Launcher.

Opens any app.

Module 21. Workspace.

Opens multiple apps side by side.

